   Overall, none of our Pockels coefficients computed with PBEsol, with or without di-
electric tensor correction, perfectly agrees with other theoretical calculations [21, 24], or
experimental values [15]. Kim et al. [24] obtained a large negative r13 = -24.3 pm/V, but
r33 and r51 coefficients matching with experiments. They carried out DFPT calculations of
four individual titanium-displaced BTO unit cells with rhombohedral geometry constrained
in a tetragonal lattice, using LDA and a corrected dielectric tensor. They averaged the
EO response of each of these four unit cells to produce a macroscopic Pockels tensor for
tetragonal BTO. On the other hand, the work of Veithen et al. [21] reported a positive
r13 = 12.7 pm/V with accuracy within experimental uncertainty, r33 = 30.8 pm/V, but no
r51 . They relied on DFPT calculations of a high-symmetry P4mm unit cell as well as on
LDA and a corrected dielectric tensor. Discrepancies between the alternate sign of r13 and
the (in)ability of calculating r51 suggest that both the choice of XC-functional and local
titanium off-centering have a significant impact on the Pockels tensor of BTO. The former
point is discussed below, whereas the latter will be addressed in Section III C.
   The observed discrepancies between our results with PBEsol and those based on LDA
in the literature (see Fig. 4) suggest that there might exist another XC-functional that
describes the EO properties of tetragonal BTO in a more accurate way than with either
LDA or PBEsol. Knowing that correcting the dielectric tensor slightly improves the Pockels
coefficient, it appears natural to choose a functional that performs well in reproducing this
property. In their study, Kim et al. corrected the dielectric tensor by opening the bandgap
of BTO with a scissor operator and obtained very good agreement with experimental data
for r33 . Instead of Eg = 1.88 eV as in our case, they used a corrected value that matched the
experimental bandgap Eg =3.33 eV and corresponds to a 56.5% increase compared to our
calculations with the PBEsol functional. Hence, a functional that corrects both the bandgap
and the dielectric tensor might improve the accuracy of Pockels coefficient calculations.
   Thanks to the functional-independent capabilities of Vibroscopy, such corrections can be
implemented in our workflow in a straightforward manner. Here, we opted for the extended
Hubbard model, which removes self-interaction errors by imposing piecewise linearity in the
energy functional as a function of occupation [47]. We selected this model because the U
and V parameters can be determined self-consistently with DFPT [48] from first-principles,

