soft phonon modes of the PBEsol+U +V model. The calculated frequency of these modes,
3.16 THz, largely exceeds experimental measurements (1.14 THz) and results with PBEsol
(1.80 THz), as can be seen in Table III). Crucially, the PBEsol+U +V phonon eigenvectors
are fundamentally different from the PBEsol ones, which considerably affects the Raman
susceptibilities and mode polarities defined in Eqs. (5) and (6), respectively. In particular,
the PBEsol+U +V eigenvectors associated with the soft modes are no longer of the Slater
type observed experimentally in [54].
   The comparison of rel and rion calculated with PBEsol and PBEsol+U +V in Fig. 5 shows
that, contrary to expectations, the electronic component (rel ) of r13 is not really affected
by the Hubbard correction. The improvement comes from rion , which becomes positive, in
the order of 7.8 pm/V, while it should not have been impacted by the presence of the U
and V terms. The same issue (variations of rion instead of rel ) occurs for r33 and r51 , thus
explaining why their value rather deteriorates instead of improving.
   To understand the origin of the rion variability with respect to the XC-functional, we
further examine the phonon contribution to rion , i.e., νijk , by excluding the dielectric tensor
renormalization. We decompose νijk into a product of Raman susceptibilities, mode polari-
