   In reality, BTO thin-films are affected by point and extended defects, as well as by the
presence of ferroelectric domains [9, 12]. Hence, the experimental Pockels coefficients of
real BTO monocrystals are expected to be smaller than those calculated from DFT on a
pristine cell. However, quantification of these non-ideal factors goes beyond the scope of
this paper. Similarly, it should be mentioned that the influence of other factors such as
the vibrational activity or finite temperature effects, should not be ruled out a priori. As
discussed in Section III A, r51 has a predominant vibrational character, driven by soft phonon
modes. In addition, the standard DFT is a 0 K theory, neglecting the thermal excitations
of the nuclei [61]. Thus, both effects could affect the value of the Pockels coefficients, as
demonstrated by Veithen et al. [21]. In a subsequent publication [62], the same authors
calculated r51 as a function of temperature. They used an effective Hamiltonian and a low-
order Taylor expansion of the energy around the paraelectric phase, with ionic degrees of
freedom aligned with the unstable phonon eigenvectors. In this configuration, they obtained

                                            17
an unclamped r51 of 622 pm/V at room temperature, including piezoelectric contributions.
While encouraging, these results severely underestimate the experimental unclamped value
of 1500 pm/V when piezoelectric contributions are taken into account. Finally, the Pockels
response is a frequency-dependent quantity [12], as the dielectric response. However, in this
work, we do not consider these frequency dependencies because they are computationally
too expensive to account for at the ab initio level. We sample Pockels responses at lattice
(phonons) and electronic resonances, and sum up individual contributions. This approach
has the advantage of being less intensive, and is valid as long as the electric field modulation
frequency is smaller than the lowest optical phonon mode frequency (i.e., 1.14 THz, see
Table III). This is the case in high-speed, GHz electro-optical modulators [14].



   C.   Pockels coefficients and local titanium off-centering


   By replacing the dielectric tensor with its experimental value, we still underestimate the
experimental r51 by a factor of 1.6 (436 vs. 700 pm/V). Correcting first-order dielectric
susceptibilities is thus insufficient. As discussed in Section III A, the vibrational properties,
and in particular the soft phonon modes, are very sensitive to local titanium off-centering.
As a consequence, Veithen et al. [21] could not report any r51 using the high-symmetry
P4mm structure and the LDA XC-functional because of the presence of imaginary phonon
modes. The inadequacy of this structure was further confirmed by Kim et al. [24], who
stressed that a cell with local titanium disorder mediated by unstable phonon modes is
required to calculate r51 . Although we use such a structure, our results do not agree with
experiments nor with Kim et al..
   One parameter that has not yet been explored in our study is the degree of titanium
off-centering applied to the P4bm supercell and how it affects the Pockels tensor. To shed
light on this phenomenon, we created a series of P4bm structures, where titanium was in-
crementally displaced along the ⟨110⟩ directions, as illustrated in Fig. 7. A 0.0% (0.466%)
displacement of titanium atoms along the ⟨110⟩ diagonals of the 2 × 2 × 1 supercell corre-
sponds to the P4mm, high-symmetry structure (structural prototype shown in Fig. 2), while
100% means that the positions of the titanium atoms coincide with those of the barium ones.
   Following the methodology of Section III B, we recalculated r13 , r33 , and r51 with PBEsol
